\alias{mlr_callback_set.resume}
\alias{CallbackSetResume}
\title{Resume Callback}
\description{
Continues training from a checkpoint, also in a new R session.
The network and optimizer states are loaded before the first epoch, and training then continues
until the learner's \code{epochs} are reached.

The \code{epochs} parameter of the learner is the \emph{total} number of epochs, i.e. it includes the
epochs that were already trained.
Resuming a checkpoint that was written after 5 epochs with a learner that is configured with
\code{epochs = 8} therefore trains 3 more epochs.
Nothing is trained if the checkpoint is already at (or beyond) the configured \code{epochs}.
}
\section{Which checkpoint is used}{

Usually, \code{path} is set to the folder that a \code{\link{CallbackSetCheckpoint}} wrote to and the most
recent checkpoint in it is used.
If that folder contains no checkpoint -- as in the first run of a script that is meant to be
restarted -- training simply starts from scratch, so that the same script can be used to start
and to continue a run.

Alternatively, \code{network_path} and \code{optimizer_path} can point to specific files, which is useful
for checkpoints that were not written by \code{\link{CallbackSetCheckpoint}}.
These files must exist.
}

\section{Callback States}{

Checkpoints written by \code{\link{CallbackSetCheckpoint}} also contain the states of the other callbacks,
which are restored into the callbacks of the same id.
This means that e.g. the training history of the previous run is preserved.
Callbacks are executed in the order in which they are passed to the learner, so this callback
should be passed \emph{first} if another callback overwrites its own state in its \code{on_begin()} stage.

Callbacks that do not implement \verb{$load_state_dict()} are skipped with a warning, as are the
states of callbacks that are not part of the current training run.

Note that schedules which are defined over the \emph{total} number of steps -- most notably
\code{\link{mlr_callback_set.lr_scheduler_one_cycle}} -- are restored as they were configured in the
interrupted run.
Configure the resuming run with the same \code{epochs} as the original one, otherwise the restored
schedule and the number of remaining steps disagree; this is checked before the first epoch is
trained and raises an error.
}

